insert into public.posts(title,slug,category,excerpt,body,hero_image_url,status,is_expert_content,published_at) values
('Spiti on Two Wheels — A Journey of a Lifetime','spiti-on-two-wheels','Ride story','What the high road teaches about preparation, pace and asking locals before pressing on.','Spiti rewards patience more than speed. Begin with weather, fuel and road-condition checks, keep margin for delays and treat local warnings as useful intelligence. Pack light without improvising on hydration, warm layers, basic tools or puncture support.','/images/community-post-1.jpg','published',false,now()),
('Chain Maintenance Guide for Long Tours','chain-maintenance-long-tours','Maintenance','A simple inspection rhythm that prevents a neglected chain from defining your tour.','Use the motorcycle and chain manufacturer instructions for products, lubrication and slack. Inspect for tight spots, damaged links, hooked teeth and unusual noise. If adjustment is unfamiliar, use a qualified technician.','/images/community-post-2.jpg','published',true,now()),
('Prepare Your Adventure Bike for the Long Road','prepare-your-adventure-bike','Expert advice','Turn preparation into a short repeatable system instead of a last-night rush.','Confirm the service schedule, tyre condition, brake feel, fluids, lights, fasteners and luggage mounts. Test the loaded motorcycle before departure and carry only tools you understand.','/images/community-post-3.jpg','published',true,now()),
('Monsoon Riding: Stay Safe and Stay on the Road','monsoon-riding','Riding tips','Visibility, smooth control and enough time matter more than maintaining a dry-road pace.','Reduce speed before difficult sections, increase following distance and use progressive controls. Give painted markings, metal covers, pooled water and repairs extra space. Stop safely when visibility is inadequate.','/images/rider-community-generated.jpg','published',true,now()),
('Tyre Pressure: Why the Cold Check Matters','motorcycle-tyre-pressure-cold-check','Maintenance','Pressure changes handling, braking feel and tyre life—so measure it consistently.','Use the motorcycle manufacturer pressure for your load and use. Check cold before a long ride, inspect the valve and tread, and investigate recurring pressure loss rather than repeatedly topping it up.','/images/blog-tyre-pressure.jpg','published',true,now()),
('The Five-Minute Pre-Ride Check','five-minute-pre-ride-check','Riding tips','A quick walk-around can reveal the small problem that would become a large one on the road.','Review tyres and wheels, controls, lights, fluids, chassis and stands. If a safety-critical item feels wrong, pause the ride and seek qualified help.','/images/blog-preride-check.jpg','published',true,now()),
('A Better Group Ride Starts Before Engines Start','group-ride-briefing','Community','Agree on pace, stops and what happens when the group separates before anyone rolls away.','Brief the route, fuel and rest stops, lead and sweep riders, communication and regrouping plan. Keep groups manageable and never pressure someone to ride beyond their ability.','/images/blog-group-riding.jpg','published',true,now()),
('A Touring Kit That Earns Its Space','touring-kit-that-earns-its-space','Touring','Pack for likely problems, your actual skills and the conditions—not for photographs.','Prioritise documents, hydration, weather protection, first aid, communication and a compact tool or repair kit suited to the motorcycle. Secure loads and recheck them after the first stop.','/images/blog-touring-kit.jpg','published',true,now())
